##: {{sense|ridicule}} {{synonyms|en|taunt|Thesaurus:deride}}
##* {{RQ:Udall Ralph Roister Doister|page=36|passage=No man for deſpite, / By worde or by write / His felowe to '''twite''', {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Calvin Golding Iob|chapter=The CXXXV. Sermon, which is the Eight vppon the .XXXIIIJ. Chapter|page=636|column=1|passage=[I]f vvee meane to '''tvvit''' a man that he is a foole, vve vvill ſay thou knovveſt not vvhat thou ſayeſt.}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-2|act=III|scene=i|page=131|column=2|passage=Hath he not '''tvvit''' our Soueraigne Lady here / VVith ignominious vvords, though Clarkely coucht? / As if ſhe had ſuborned ſome to ſvveare / Falſe allegations, to o'rethrovv his ſtate.}}
##* {{RQ:Drayton Poemes Lyrick and Pastorall|eglog=2|page=39|passage=Well wanton, laugh not my ould age to ſcorne, / nor '''twit''' me ſo my ſenſes to haue loſte, / the time hath been when as my hopefull morne / promiſ'd as much as nowe thy youth can boaſte: {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Fuller Palestine|chapter=The Tribe of {{w|Ephraim}}|paragraph=18|page=186|passage=This ''[[w:Hannah (Bible)|Hannah]]'' though ſilent vvhen '''tvvitted''' by ''{{w|Peninnah}}'' for barrenneſs, found her tongue vvhen here taxed by ''[[w:Eli (Bible)|Eli]]'' of drunkenneſs: becauſe a meer ſufferer in the former, but in the latter a ſinner, had the accuſation been true.}}
##* {{RQ:Rabelais Gargantua|volume=I|book=3|chapter=How Friar Jhon Comforteth Panurge in the Doubtful Matter of Cuckoldry|page=438|passage=Thou '''twittest''' me with my Grey Hairs, yet considerest not how I am of the Nature of Leeks, which with a white Head carry a green, fresh, streight, and vigorous Tail.}}
##* {{RQ:Fuller Iniured Innocence|part=I|chapter=That the Author Designed unto Himself No Party-pleasing in Writing His Church-history|page=11|passage=The old ''Non-conformiſts'' being the ſame vvith the modern ''Presbuterians'', but ''depreſſed and under'', as the modern ''Presbuterians'' are the old ''Non-conformiſts'', but ''vertical'' and in ''Authority'', do (though the ''Animadvertor'' '''tvvitteth''' me conſtantly to Advocate for them) take great and general exception at me; {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:More Iniquity|chapter=VI|page=19|passage=Again in the [[w:Books of Chronicles|ſecond Book of the Chronicles]], there ''[[w:Abijah of Judah|Abijah]]'' King of ''Juda'' ſpeaking to ''Iſrael'', upbraids to them their confidence ''in their multitude'', and ''in their Golden Calves vvhich {{w|Jeroboam}} made them for Gods''; '''tvvitting''' them thereby aſſuredly for their ''Idolatry''; {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:L'Estrange Fables of Aesop|section=Preface|page=12|passage=Hovv much are vve Oblig'd then, {{...}} to ''[[w:Aesop|Æſop]]'', in the Firſt Place, as the ''Founder'', and ''Original Author'', or ''Inventer'' of This Art of Schooling Mankind into Better Manners; by Minding Men of their Errors vvithout '''Tvvitting''' them for what's Amiſs, and by That Means Flaſhing the Light of their Ovvn Conſciences in their Ovvn Faces!}}
##* {{RQ:Tillotson Works|page=101|passage=Novv this, 'tis probable, theſe ''Scoffers'' '''tvvitted''' the Chriſtians vvithall; And becauſe [[w:Jesus|Chriſt]] did not come vvhen ſome looked for him, they concluded he vvould not come at all.}}
##* {{RQ:Boswell Johnson|year=1775|page=515|passage=VVhen on a ſubſequent day, he vvas '''tvvitted''' by Mrs. Thrale for being very late, vvhich he generally vvas, he defended himſelf by alluding to the extraordinary morning, vvhen he had been too early, "Madam, I do not like to come down to ''vacuity''."}}
##* {{RQ:Lamb Essays of Elia|chapter=A Complaint of the Decay of Beggars in the Metropolis|pages=265–266|pageref=266|passage=No rascally comparative insults a Beggar, or thinks of weighing purses with him. {{...}} No one '''twitteth''' him with ostentation above his means. No one accuses him of pride, or upbraideth him with mock humility.}}
##* {{RQ:Baillie Dramas|volume=I|act=III|scene=ii|page=55|passage=Nay, do not '''twit''' me now with all the freaks, / And levities, and gambols charged upon me / By every lean-faced dame that wears a hood.}}
##* {{RQ:Melville Pierre|book=XVIII|section=II|page=356|passage=Now some imaginatively heterodoxical men are often surprisingly '''twitted''' upon their willful inverting of all common-sense notions, their absurd and all-displacing transcendentals, which say three is four, and two and two make ten.}}
##* {{RQ:Trollope Belton Estate|volume=I|chapter=Captain Aylmer Meets His Constituents|page=193|passage=Anything would be better than being '''twitted''' in this way. How can I help it that I am not a man and able to work for my bread?}}
##* {{quote-hansard|en|speaker=[[w:John Williamson (New Zealand politician)|John Williamson]]|author2=Maurice Fitzgerald, compiler|debate=Separation. Adjourned Debate.|report=New Zealand. Parliamentary Debates. Third and Fourth Parliaments.{{nb...|Legislative Council and House of Representatives. 1864 to 1866.}}|house=[[w:New Zealand House of Representatives|House of Representatives]]|location=Wellington|publisher=G. Didsbury, government printer|date=12 September 1865|year_published=1887|page=499|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=CK0AAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA499|column=2|oclc=84941993|passage=The honourable member for Ellesmere, on the opening of this session, got up and '''twitted''' them with not being there on the first day.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|title=‘Masterly Inactivity’|journal=[[w:Punch (magazine)|Punch, or The London Charivari]]|location=London|publisher={{...|Published at the office, 85, Fleet Street, and sold by all booksellers.}} [[w:Bradbury and Evans|Bradbury, Agnew, & Co.]],{{nb...|Ld., printers, Whitefriars.}}|date=14 October 1893|volume=CV|page=174|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/punchvol104a105lemouoft/punchvol104a105lemouoft/page/174/mode/1up|column=2|oclc=732224722|passage=Dear France, thou thy insular neighbour oft '''twittest''' / As "Shopkeeper!" Well ma'am, ''j'y suis'', and shall stop; / For a Shopkeeper's one who—of course—''keeps the Shop''!}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Rex Stout|chapter=w:When a Man Murders|title=w:The American Magazine|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=Colver Publishing House|month=May|year=1954|volume=157|page=128|columns=1–2|issn=2155-7225|oclc=6403725|newversion=republished in|title2=[[w:Three Witnesses (book)|Three Witnesses]]|location2=New York, N.Y.|publisher2=w:Bantam Books|year2=October 1994|page2=106|isbn2=978-0-553-24959-0|passage=Mr. Cramer, a policeman, came this morning and '''twitted''' me for having let a murderer hoodwink me.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|title=Talking of Trains: Under Their Hats|journal=w:Modern Railways|location=Shepperton, Surrey|publisher=w:Ian Allan Publishing|month=August|year=1962|page=80|issn=0026-8356|oclc=884584817|passage=Secrecy about B.R. plans for reorganisation and closure of lines and notably some failures to consult with staff representatives concerned with redundancy, are defects with which the railway unions have '''twitted''' Dr. Beeching.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|author=w:Bernard Porter|title=Did He Puff His Crimes to Please a Bloodthirsty Readership? ''Stanley: The Impossible Life of Africa’s Greatest Explorer'' by {{w|Tim Jeal}}. Faber, 570 pp., £25, March 2007, 978 0 571 22102 8 [book review]|editor=w:Mary-Kay Wilmers|journal=w:London Review of Books|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20240303025848/https://www.lrb.co.uk/the-paper/v29/n07/bernard-porter/did-he-puff-his-crimes-to-please-a-bloodthirsty-readership|location=London|publisher=LRB Ltd.|date=5 April 2007|volume=29|issue=7|page=10|issn=0260-9592|oclc=848524821|passage=[[w:Henry Fox Bourne|H[enry] R[ichard] Fox Bourne]], secretary of the Aborigines' Protection Society&nbsp;– often '''twitted''' for being an 'armchair critic'&nbsp;– wrote in a review of one of [[w:Henry Morton Stanley|[Henry Morton] Stanley]]'s books: 'The Society is not condemning Mr Stanley or his subordinates so much, but the mounting of an expedition with aims and methods which almost necessitated the cruelties and slaughters that were incident to it&nbsp;… It seems better to remain in armchairs and pass resolutions than wantonly to embark on perilous enterprises, which can only be carried out by means that degrade Englishmen.'}}
